<h2 id="assess-a-repository">Assess a repository</h2>
<p>Six of the tools take <code>{ repository: "https://github.com/OWNER/REPO" }</code> (optionally with <code>ref</code>) or a saved <code>{ snapshot }</code>. They are the same pipeline as the <a href="/adopt/">Adopt</a> page and <code>jrc assess</code>:</p>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Collect.</strong> The page reads public files at one commit: repository settings, the commit id, the recursive file list and up to 24 key files. That's three API requests plus a few file reads, all <code>GET</code> with no credentials.</li>
  <li><strong>Evaluate.</strong> A pure function scores each pillar from file names and a few fixed fields, as <em>evidenced</em>, <em>partially-evidenced</em>, <em>missing</em>, <em>not-applicable</em> or <em>unknown</em>. <em>Missing</em> means "not found in this repository", never "violates JRCv1". <em>Unknown</em> means the scan couldn't tell. Demonstrated problems are listed separately as concerns.</li>
  <li><strong>Prepare.</strong> The tools produce a roadmap, a candidate <code>jrc.json</code>, a checklist, a new-files-only patch, a PR description and a challenge log. They return text and GitHub links for the developer to review and submit. No tool can sign in, open a PR or issue, or write anywhere.</li>
</ol>
<p>Repository text is untrusted. Tools never echo README or manifest prose, file names with hidden characters are dropped, and a saved snapshot is validated before it is assessed. The same snapshot always gives the same report: <code>jrc assess --snapshot jrc-snapshot.json</code> reproduces it byte for byte.</p>

